
  <sn><md># Regex Engines and Flavors

## The Big Picture

"Regex" isn't one thing. There are **families** of engines with different capabilities, syntax, and performance characteristics. Knowing which flavor you're using determines what works, what breaks, and how fast it runs.

Two orthogonal questions define any regex implementation:

1. **What engine algorithm does it use?** (how it matches)
2. **What syntax/features does it support?** (what you can write)

---

## Part 1: Engine Algorithms

### 1. DFA (Deterministic Finite Automaton)

- One active state at a time
- **Linear time** — O(n) regardless of pattern
- No backtracking → no ReDoS
- **Cannot** support backreferences or lookaround (they exceed regular languages)
- Finds *leftmost-longest* match (POSIX style)

**Used by:** `egrep` (old), RE2 in DFA mode, some grep implementations, `lex`/`flex` internally

### 2. NFA (Non-deterministic Finite Automaton)

- Can be in many states at once (simulated)
- **Linear time** — O(n × m)
- No backtracking → no ReDoS
- Supports captures (with extra bookkeeping)
- Still can't do backreferences/lookaround in pure form

**Used by:** RE2, Go's `regexp`, Rust's `regex`, Hyperscan

### 3. Backtracking (the "traditional" engine)

- Tries one path; on failure, backs up and tries another
- **Exponential worst case** → catastrophic backtracking / ReDoS
- Supports *everything*: backreferences, lookaround, recursion, atomic groups
- Match order depends on greedy/lazy choices

**Used by:** PCRE, Perl, Python `re`, JavaScript, Java `java.util.regex`, .NET, Ruby, PHP

### 4. Hybrid / Two-pass

- Run a fast DFA/NFA prefilter, then a backtracker for the hard parts
- **Used by:** PCRE2 (JIT + auto-possessification), .NET (some optimizations), V8 (Irregexp uses a backtracking engine with optimizations)

### The trade-off in one line

```
Fast &amp; safe (DFA/NFA)  ←──────────────→  Feature-rich (backtracking)
   no backrefs                              backrefs, lookaround
   linear time                              exponential risk
```

---

## Part 2: The Major Flavors

### POSIX

The original standard. Two sub-flavors:

| | BRE (Basic) | ERE (Extended) |
|---|---|---|
| `+ ? { }` | literal, need `\+` | special |
| `( )` groups | need `\(` | special |
| `\|` alternation | need `\|` | `|` |
| Used by | `grep`, `sed`, `ed` | `grep -E`, `awk`, `egrep` |

**Leftmost-longest** matching semantics. No backreferences in ERE (BRE has them). No lookaround.

### PCRE (Perl Compatible Regular Expressions)

The de facto standard for "rich" regex. Used by PHP, and the reference for many others.

- Backreferences: `\1`, `\k<name>`
- Lookahead/lookbehind: `(?=)`, `(?!)`, `(?&lt;=)`, `(?<!--)`
- Named groups: `(?<name-->...)`
- Atomic groups: `(?&gt;...)`
- Possessive quantifiers: `a*+`
- Recursion: `(?R)`, `(?1)`
- Conditionals: `(?(1)yes|no)`
- Verbs: `(*SKIP)`, `(*FAIL)`
- Unicode properties: `\p{L}`, `\p{Script=Greek}`

### Perl

The origin of most modern features. PCRE is "Perl Compatible" because Perl came first.

- All PCRE features plus Perl-specific ones
- `\K` (reset match start)
- Code assertions: `(?{ ... })`, `(??{ ... })`
- `\G` (match at pos)
- Best-in-class Unicode support

### Python (`re`, `regex`)

- `re` — stdlib, close to PCRE but missing some features
- `regex` — third-party, adds `\p{}`, variable-length lookbehind, fuzzy matching, `(?V1)` mode

Python-specific:
- No `\K`, no `(?R)` in `re`
- Fixed-width lookbehind only (in `re`)
- `re.VERBOSE`, `re.ASCII`, `re.IGNORECASE` flags
- Named groups: `(?P<name>...)`, backref `(?P=name)`

### JavaScript (ECMAScript)

- Based on a backtracking engine
- `u` flag → Unicode mode (proper `\p{}`, code point handling)
- `s` flag → dotall (`.` matches newline)
- `y` flag → sticky (match at lastIndex)
- `d` flag → indices for groups
- Lookbehind supported (since ES2018)
- Named groups `(?<name>...)` (ES2018)
- **No** atomic groups, possessive quantifiers, recursion, conditionals
- **No** inline flags except `(?i)` at start (and `(?i:...)` groups since ES2025)

### Java (`java.util.regex`)

- Backtracking, very close to PCRE
- Atomic groups, possessive quantifiers, lookbehind (bounded)
- `\Q...\E` quoting
- **No** recursion, no conditionals
- Lookbehind can be variable-length in newer Javas (bounded)

### .NET

- Backtracking, feature-rich
- **Balancing groups** `(?<open>)(?&lt;-open&gt;)` — unique to .NET
- Variable-length lookbehind
- Right-to-left matching option
- Conditionals, atomic groups, recursion-ish via balancing

### Go (`regexp`)

- RE2-derived NFA
- **Linear time**, no ReDoS
- **No** backreferences, no lookaround
- POSIX and Perl-ish syntax modes
- Great for untrusted input

### Rust (`regex`)

- NFA-based, **linear time**, no ReDoS
- **No** backreferences, no lookaround (by design)
- Unicode-first
- For lookaround/backrefs, the `fancy-regex` crate wraps a backtracker

### RE2 (Google)

- C++ library, NFA-based
- Linear time, safe for untrusted patterns
- Used by Google products, BigQuery, etc.

### Oniguruma / Onigmo

- Backtracking, very feature-rich
- Used by Ruby (Onigmo), PHP's mbstring, TextMate, some editors
- Supports many Perl features plus its own

### Vim / VSCode / Editors

- Vim has its own flavor (magic/nomagic, `\v` very magic)
- VSCode uses JS regex (with some Rust-side extensions for search)
- Sublime uses Boost.Regex (PCRE-like)

---

## Part 3: Feature Comparison Table

| Feature | POSIX ERE | PCRE | Perl | Python `re` | JS | Java | .NET | Go | Rust |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Backreferences | ✗ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✗ | ✗ |
| Lookahead | ✗ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✗ | ✗ |
| Lookbehind | ✗ | ✓ | ✓ | fixed | ✓ | bounded | variable | ✗ | ✗ |
| Named groups | ✗ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✗ | ✓ |
| Atomic groups | ✗ | ✓ | ✓ | ✗* | ✗ | ✓ | ✓ | ✗ | ✗ |
| Possessive `*+` | ✗ | ✓ | ✓ | ✗* | ✗ | ✓ | ✓ | ✗ | ✗ |
| Recursion `(?R)` | ✗ | ✓ | ✓ | ✗ | ✗ | ✗ | ✗ | ✗ | ✗ |
| Conditionals | ✗ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✗ | ✗ | ✓ | ✗ | ✗ |
| `\p{}` Unicode | ✗ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ (u) | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ |
| `\K` | ✗ | ✓ | ✓ | ✗ | ✗ | ✗ | ✗ | ✗ | ✗ |
| Linear time | ✓ | ✗ | ✗ | ✗ | ✗ | ✗ | ✗ | ✓ | ✓ |
| ReDoS safe | ✓ | ✗ | ✗ | ✗ | ✗ | ✗ | ✗ | ✓ | ✓ |

\* Python `regex` module adds these.

---

## Part 4: What This Means in Practice

### If you're writing a pattern for untrusted input

Use a **linear-time engine**: Go, Rust `regex`, RE2. They can't blow up. You give up backrefs and lookaround, but most real-world patterns don't need them.

### If you're writing a pattern that needs backrefs/lookaround

You need a **backtracking engine** (PCRE, Python, JS, Java, .NET). Now you must watch for:

- **Catastrophic backtracking** — `(a+)+b` against `aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa`
- **Nested quantifiers** — `(a*)*`
- **Overlapping alternations** — `(a|a)*`

Mitigations: atomic groups `(?&gt;...)`, possessive quantifiers `a*+`, or rewriting the pattern.

### If you're porting a pattern between languages

Expect breakage. Common gotchas:

| Pattern | Works in | Breaks in |
|---|---|---|
| `(?&lt;=a+)b` | .NET, Java (newer) | Python `re`, JS |
| `(?R)` | PCRE, Perl | JS, Java, .NET, Python |
| `\K` | PCRE, Perl | everything else |
| `(?<name>...)` | PCRE, .NET, JS, Python* | older engines |
| `\p{L}` | most | POSIX, old JS (no `u` flag) |
| Possessive `a*+` | PCRE, Java, .NET | JS, Python `re` |
| `\Q...\E` | PCRE, Java | JS, Python |
| Backrefs | PCRE, JS, Java, .NET, Python | Go, Rust, RE2 |

\* Python uses `(?P<name>...)` traditionally, `(?<name>...)` in newer versions.

### If you're choosing an engine for a tool

- **CLI search (grep-like):** RE2 or Rust `regex` for speed and safety
- **Language built-ins:** whatever your language ships (Python `re`, JS RegExp, Go `regexp`)
- **Editor:** usually JS or Oniguruma
- **Log processing at scale:** RE2 / Hyperscan
- **Need every feature:** PCRE2 or Perl

---

## Part 5: The Deeper Point

The flavor wars come down to one fundamental tension:

```
Regular languages          Extended "regex"
(finite automata)    ←────────────────────→   (Turing-ish)
• linear time                                  • backrefs, recursion
• no ReDoS                                     • exponential risk
• safe for untrusted input                     • can match non-regular sets
• DFA/NFA engines                              • backtracking engines
```

Theoretical "regex" = regular languages = DFA/NFA. Practical "regex" in PCRE/Perl/JS = a **programming language** that happens to look like regex. That's why:

- `(?R)` can match balanced parens (not regular)
- `\1` can match `aⁿbⁿ` (not regular)
- These features **require** a backtracking engine
- And backtracking is exactly what enables ReDoS

So "which flavor" is really: **do you want the safety and speed of regular languages, or the power (and danger) of a Turing-flavored pattern language?**

---

## Summary

| Question | Answer |
|---|---|
| What are the engine types? | DFA, NFA, backtracking, hybrid |
| Which is fast/safe? | DFA/NFA (linear time, no ReDoS) |
| Which is feature-rich? | Backtracking (PCRE, Perl, JS, Java, .NET) |
| What are the main flavors? | POSIX, PCRE, Perl, Python, JS, Java, .NET, Go, Rust, Oniguruma, RE2 |
| Why do patterns break when ported? | Different feature sets and semantics per flavor |
| What's the core trade-off? | Regular-language safety vs. extended-feature power |
| Which should I use? | Match the engine to your threat model and feature needs — untrusted input → linear engine; exotic features → backtracker with care |</name></name></name></open></name></name></name></md></sn>
